@inject Motion Motion
@* The list of things already on the sheet, holding <ChosenRow> children.

   A row arriving here should land rather than appear — it is the one thing in this app that
   reads as an object moving from the picker below into the character above, and the only user
   of `--ease-emphasised`.

   **Which row is new is decided by a `data-landed` mark in `motion.js` rather than by a key
   here**, because a key would have to be threaded through every call site: `ChosenRow` carries
   no identity today, and the eight components that build these lists would each have to supply
   one that is stable across a re-render. The mark needs nothing from them. Blazor also reuses
   DOM nodes across renders, so the render tree does not cheaply answer "which of these is new"
   in the first place. *@

<ul class="chosen" @ref="_list">
    @ChildContent
</ul>

@code {
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    private ElementReference? _list;

    /// <summary>
    /// Let any row that has not been seen before land.
    ///
    /// <para><b><c>firstRender</c> is passed through rather than used to skip the call.</b> On a
    /// first render the rows are marked but not animated, so restoring a saved character does not
    /// play twelve arrivals at once — but they must still be marked, or the next genuine addition
    /// lands the entire list with it.</para>
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (_list is not { } list) return;

        await Motion.Land(list, !firstRender);
    }
}
